**How the tests work:** `operations` lists the method names called in order, and `arguments` gives each call's arguments. The expected output lists each call's return value, with `null` for methods that return nothing.

**Follow-up:** can you do it with just one queue?$b$, $b${"python": "class MyStack:\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        pass\n\n    def push(self, x: int) -> None:\n        pass\n\n    def pop(self) -> int:\n        pass\n\n    def top(self) -> int:\n        pass\n\n    def empty(self) -> bool:\n        pass\n", "javascript": "class MyStack {\n    constructor() {\n        \n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @param {number} x\n     * @return {void}\n     */\n    push(x) {\n        \n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @return {number}\n     */\n    pop() {\n        \n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @return {number}\n     */\n    top() {\n        \n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @return {boolean}\n     */\n    empty() {\n        \n    }\n}\n"}$b$::jsonb, '{stack,queue,design}', 21, array[$b$operations$b$, $b$arguments$b$]::text[], 'exact')
